;; Docs for the core of std/prelude: arithmetic, bits, printing, boxes, the
;; ref traits, Option and Result, parameters, equality's helpers and failure.
;; Read through (:doc #:file ...). The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose
;; is @code{...}.

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Arithmetic
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc +
  (summary "The sum of two numbers.")
  (arg a "The first addend.")
  (arg b "The second, of the same type.")
  (returns "a plus b. An integer sum that overflows wraps.")
  (see - * negate)
  (example """
    (+ 1 2)       ; 3
    #fl(+ 1 2 3)     ; 6, folded from the left
    (+)           ; 0
    """)
  (reference """
    The C# operator, at any type with a @code{Num} implementation. Written
    with more than two operands it folds from the left; @code{(+ x)} is
    @code{x} and @code{(+)} is 0.

    Nothing checks for overflow at run time: @code{(+ n 1)} at
    @code{int} 2147483647 is -2147483648. Widen first where a sum must not
    overflow. There is no implicit widening between variables, so an
    @code{int} and a @code{double} need a @code{cast} to be added.
    """))

(:doc -
  (summary "The difference of two numbers.")
  (arg a "What to subtract from.")
  (arg b "What to subtract, of the same type.")
  (returns "a minus b. An integer difference that overflows wraps.")
  (see + negate)
  (example """
    (- 10 3)      ; 7
    #fl(- 10 3 2)    ; 5, folded from the left
    (- 4)         ; -4, which is (negate 4)
    """)
  (reference """
    The C# operator. With more than two operands it folds from the left. With
    one, it is @code{negate}, which keeps the operand's type.
    """))

(:doc *
  (summary "The product of two numbers.")
  (arg a "The first factor.")
  (arg b "The second, of the same type.")
  (returns "a times b. An integer product that overflows wraps.")
  (see + /)
  (example """
    (* 6 7)       ; 42
    #fl(* 2 3 4)     ; 24
    (*)           ; 1
    """)
  (reference """
    The C# operator. With more than two operands it folds from the left;
    @code{(* x)} is @code{x} and @code{(*)} is 1.
    """))

(:doc /
  (summary "The quotient of two numbers, truncated at an integer type.")
  (arg a "The dividend.")
  (arg b "The divisor, of the same type.")
  (returns "a divided by b, rounded toward zero for integers.")
  (raises System.DivideByZeroException "When b is an integer zero.")
  (see % recip *)
  (example """
    (/ 7 2)       ; 3
    (/ 7.0 2.0)   ; 3.5
    (/ 1.0 0.0)   ; infinity
    #fl(/ 100 5 2)   ; 10
    """)
  (reference """
    The C# operator. Integer division truncates and throws on a zero
    divisor; a constant integer divided by the literal 0 is refused at
    compile time. Double division by zero is infinite, and
    @code{(/ 0.0 0.0)} is NaN.

    With more than two operands it folds from the left. With one, it is
    @code{recip}.
    """))

(:doc %
  (summary "The remainder of a division, with the sign of the dividend.")
  (arg a "The dividend.")
  (arg b "The divisor, of the same type.")
  (returns "What is left of a after dividing by b.")
  (raises System.DivideByZeroException "When b is an integer zero.")
  (see /)
  (example """
    (% 7 2)       ; 1
    (% -7 2)      ; -1, not 1
    (% 7 -2)      ; 1
    """)
  (reference """
    The C# operator: a remainder, not a modulus. The always-positive one is
    @code{(% (+ (% a n) n) n)}.
    """))

(:doc negate
  (summary "A number with its sign flipped.")
  (arg x "The number.")
  (returns "Minus x, of x's own type.")
  (see - recip)
  (reference """
    @code{(negate x)} is the same as @code{(- x)}, since @code{(- x)} expands to thi function.
    A primitive rather than @code{(- 0 x)}, which would fix the type to that of the literal 0 and so
    refuse a @code{double}. It is C#'s unary minus, so negating the most
    negative integer of a type wraps to itself.
    """))

(:doc recip
  (summary "The reciprocal of a number: one divided by it.")
  (arg x "The number.")
  (returns "1 divided by x, of x's own type.")
  (see / negate)
  (example """
    (recip 4.0)   ; 0.25
    (/ 4.0)       ; the same
    (recip 4)     ; 0: integer division
    """)
  (reference """
    What @code{(/ x)} expands to, emitted as @code{1 / x} at x's type. At
    an integer type that is integer division, so it is 0 for any x other
    than 1 and -1, and throws @code{System.DivideByZeroException} for 0.
    """))

(:doc sqrt
  (summary "The square root of a number.")
  (arg x "The number. An integer argument is widened to a double.")
  (returns "The non-negative square root, as a double. NaN for a negative x.")
  (see * recip)
  (example """
    (sqrt 9)      ; 3.0
    (sqrt 2.0)    ; 1.4142135623730951
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{System.Math.Sqrt}. The rest of @code{System.Math} is in
    @code{(std maths)}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Comparison
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc <
  (summary "Whether one number is less than another.")
  (arg a "The first operand.")
  (arg b "The second, of the same type.")
  (returns "#t when a is less than b. #f when either is NaN.")
  (see <= > >= compare)
  (example """
    (< 1 2)          ; #t
    #ch(< 1 2 3)     ; #t: each is less than the next
    """)
  (reference """
    The C# operator, at numbers, @code{char} and @code{StringCursor}: the
    types with an @code{Ordered} implementation. Not at @code{string}, which
    has no such operator; @code{compare} and @code{less?} order every
    comparable type, strings ordinally.

    For chaining, use the #ch(< 1 2 3) hash extension, comparing each operand
    with the next. Comparisons follow IEEE, so one against NaN is #f
    whichever way round it is written.
    """))

(:doc <=
  (summary "Whether one number is less than or equal to another.")
  (arg a "The first operand.")
  (arg b "The second, of the same type.")
  (returns "#t when a is at most b. #f when either is NaN.")
  (see < >= at-most?)
  (reference """
    The C# operator, as @code{<} is. With more than two operands it chains.
    """))

(:doc >
  (summary "Whether one number is greater than another.")
  (arg a "The first operand.")
  (arg b "The second, of the same type.")
  (returns "#t when a is greater than b. #f when either is NaN.")
  (see >= < greater?)
  (reference """
    The C# operator, as @code{<} is.
    """))

(:doc >=
  (summary "Whether one number is greater than or equal to another.")
  (arg a "The first operand.")
  (arg b "The second, of the same type.")
  (returns "#t when a is at least b. #f when either is NaN.")
  (see > <= at-least?)
  (reference """
    The C# operator, as @code{<} is.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Bits
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc bitwise-and
  (summary "The bits set in both of two integers.")
  (arg a "The first integer.")
  (arg b "The second, of the same type.")
  (returns "a AND b, bit by bit.")
  (see bitwise-ior bitwise-xor bitwise-not)
  (example """
    (bitwise-and 12 10)   ; 8
    """)
  (reference """
    C#'s @code{&}, at every integer type, signed and unsigned: the types with
    an @code{Integral} implementation. With more than two operands it folds
    from the left.
    """))

(:doc bitwise-ior
  (summary "The bits set in either of two integers.")
  (arg a "The first integer.")
  (arg b "The second, of the same type.")
  (returns "a OR b, bit by bit.")
  (see bitwise-and bitwise-xor)
  (example """
    (bitwise-ior 12 10)   ; 14
    """)
  (reference """
    C#'s @code{|}, at every integer type.
    """))

(:doc bitwise-xor
  (summary "The bits set in exactly one of two integers.")
  (arg a "The first integer.")
  (arg b "The second, of the same type.")
  (returns "a XOR b, bit by bit.")
  (see bitwise-and bitwise-ior)
  (example """
    (bitwise-xor 12 10)   ; 6
    """)
  (reference """
    C#'s @code{^}, at every integer type.
    """))

(:doc bitwise-not
  (summary "An integer with every bit flipped.")
  (arg x "The integer.")
  (returns "The complement of x, of x's type.")
  (see bitwise-and bitwise-xor)
  (example """
    (bitwise-not 0)       ; -1
    """)
  (reference """
    C#'s @code{~}, at every integer type.
    """))

(:doc shift-left
  (summary "An integer's bits moved toward the high end.")
  (arg x "The integer to shift.")
  (arg n "How many places. An int whatever x is, masked to x's width.")
  (returns "x shifted left by n, zeroes filling in from the low end.")
  (see shift-right shift-right-logical)
  (example """
    (shift-left 1 4)      ; 16
    (shift-left 1 32)     ; 1, not 0: the count is masked
    """)
  (reference """
    C#'s @code{<<}. C# masks the count to the operand's width, so at an
    @code{int} a count of 32 shifts by 0.
    """))

(:doc shift-right
  (summary "An integer's bits moved toward the low end, keeping the sign of a signed type.")
  (arg x "The integer to shift.")
  (arg n "How many places. An int whatever x is, masked to x's width.")
  (returns "x shifted right by n.")
  (see shift-right-logical shift-left)
  (example """
    (shift-right -16 2)   ; -4
    """)
  (reference """
    C#'s @code{>>}: arithmetic on a signed type, copying the sign bit in,
    and logical on an unsigned one. The count is masked as for
    @code{shift-left}.
    """))

(:doc shift-right-logical
  (summary "An integer's bits moved toward the low end, zeroes filling in.")
  (arg x "The integer to shift.")
  (arg n "How many places. An int whatever x is, masked to x's width.")
  (returns "x shifted right by n, with zeroes in the high bits whatever x's sign.")
  (see shift-right shift-left)
  (example """
    (shift-right-logical -1 28)   ; 15
    """)
  (reference """
    C#'s @code{>>>}. The count is masked as for @code{shift-left}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Numbers to strings
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc int->string
  (summary "The decimal text of an int, the same on every machine.")
  (arg n "The number.")
  (returns "Its digits, with an ASCII minus when negative.")
  (see string->int long->string ->str)
  (reference """
    Does not use the c# culture, where minus can come back with U+2212 MINUS SIGN.
    The result is guaranteed to round-trip through string->int. 
    """))

(:doc long->string
  (summary "The decimal text of a long, the same on every machine.")
  (arg n "The number.")
  (returns "Its digits, with an ASCII minus when negative.")
  (see int->string ->str))

(:doc byte->string
  (summary "The decimal text of a byte.")
  (arg n "The number.")
  (returns "Its digits, from 0 to 255.")
  (see int->string ->str))

(:doc double->string
  (summary "The text of a double, the same on every machine.")
  (arg n "The number.")
  (returns "The shortest text that reads back as the same double, with a point as the decimal separator.")
  (see string->double int->string ->str)
  (example """
    (double->string 6.0)   ; "6"
    (double->string 2.5)   ; "2.5"
    """)
  (reference """
    .NET's default formatting in the invariant culture, so it round-trips
    through @code{string->double} on any machine. A whole number has no
    point: 6.0 is written @code{6}. Use @code{(std fmt)} when the shape of
    the output matters.
    """))

(:doc number->string
  (summary "The decimal text of an int, under the name R7RS uses.")
  (arg n "The number.")
  (returns "The string representation of n, identical to int->string.")
  (see int->string ->str)
  (reference """
    Only for @code{int}. Other numeric types have their own conversions, or
    @code{->str}.
    """))

(:doc clr-string->string
  (summary "A Bjolang string from a .NET System.String.")
  (arg s "The .NET string. Null is allowed.")
  (returns "The same text as a Bjolang string; the empty string for null.")
  (see string->clr-string)
  (reference """
    A .NET member that takes or returns a @code{System.String} directly is
    converted at the call. This is for one nested in another type, such as
    a @code{(Seq System.String)}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Printing
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc ->str
  (summary "The text a value prints as.")
  (arg x "The value.")
  (returns "Its text, as print and println write it.")
  (see print println list->str int->string)
  (example """
    (->str 42)                ; "42"
    (->str #\a)               ; "a"
    (->str (list 1 2 3))      ; "(1 2 3)"
    (->str [1 2])             ; "[1 2]"
    (->str (Some "x"))        ; "(Some x)"
    """)
  (reference """
    The method of the @code{->str} trait. Implemented for @code{string},
    which is itself, @code{int}, @code{long}, @code{double} and
    @code{byte}, through the invariant conversions such as
    @code{int->string}, and for @code{char}, @code{Keyword} and
    @code{Symbol}. A @code{char} prints as itself, not as @code{#\a}.

    The containers print their elements through the elements' own
    @code{->str}: a @code{List} as @code{(1 2 3)}, a @code{Vec} as
    @code{[1 2 3]}, an @code{Array} as @code{#[1 2 3]}, a @code{Map} as
    @code{#map((k v) ...)}, an @code{Option} as @code{(Some x)} or
    @code{None}, a @code{Result} as @code{(Ok x)} or @code{(Err e)}, and a
    @code{Box} as @code{(Box x)}. A @code{Seq} prints as @code{(Seq ...)},
    without being walked.

    Anything with no implementation of its own falls to .NET's
    @code{ToString}, asked in the invariant culture.
    """))

(:doc print
  (summary "Writes a value's text to the current output port.")
  (arg x "The value, written through its ->str.")
  (see println ->str display)
  (reference """
    @code{(display (->str x))}: no newline after it. The port is
    @code{current-output-port}, so @code{parameterize} redirects it.
    """))

(:doc println
  (summary "Writes a value's text to the current output port, then a newline.")
  (arg x "The value, written through its ->str.")
  (see print ->str displayln)
  (example """
    (println 42)          ; 42
    (println [1 2 3])     ; [1 2 3]
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{(displayln (->str x))}. The port is @code{current-output-port}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Unit and discarding
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc unit
  (summary "The single value of type Unit.")
  (see ignore)
  (reference """
    Used when an expression or function body must evaluate to @code{Unit}
    without performing meaningful work, such as in @code{Discard}'s blanket
    implementation. Equivalent to @code{(begin)}. Not to be confused with
    @code{(Tuple)}, which is the empty tuple.
    """))

(:doc ignore
  (summary "Discards a value that would otherwise have to be used.")
  (arg x "The value.")
  (see unit detach)
  (example """
    (ignore (map-try-ref m k))
    (ignore (bjo (log-visit user)))   ; detaches the promise
    """)
  (reference """
    The method of the @code{Discard} trait. An expression in statement
    position must evaluate to @code{Unit}; any other type (including a
    @code{Result} or a .NET method return value) will trigger a compiler
    error. Use @code{ignore} to explicitly discard unwanted values.
    For most types it does nothing.

    On a @code{Promise} it is @code{detach}: the result is not wanted, but
    a failure inside the fiber is reported as unhandled rather than lost
    silently. A fiber that stopped because it was cancelled is not
    reported.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Boxes
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Box
  (summary "A mutable cell holding one value.")
  (tparam %a "What it holds.")
  (see make-box box-ref box-set! deref)
  (example """
    (def count (make-box 0))
    (box-set! count (+ (box-ref count) 1))
    (deref count)   ; 1
    """)
  (reference """
    A one-element array behind an opaque struct. Copying a box copies the
    reference to the array, so every copy reads and writes the same cell.
    It has a @code{Derefable} implementation, so @code{deref} and
    @code{get} read it, and prints as @code{(Box x)}.
    """))

(:doc make-box
  (summary "A new box holding a value.")
  (arg elem "What it starts out holding.")
  (returns "A box of its own.")
  (see box-ref box-set!))

(:doc box-ref
  (summary "What a box holds.")
  (arg b "The box.")
  (returns "The value most recently put in it.")
  (see box-set! deref))

(:doc box-set!
  (summary "Replaces what a box holds, in place.")
  (arg box "The box.")
  (arg val "Its new value.")
  (see box-ref make-box))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Reading and writing by key
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc ref
  (summary "Looks up an element in a collection by index or key.")
  (arg coll "The collection to index.")
  (arg i "The lookup key or zero-based integer index.")
  (returns "The element associated with i.")
  (raises System.IndexOutOfRangeException "When coll is an Array or Vec and i is out of bounds.")
  (raises System.Collections.Generic.KeyNotFoundException "When coll is a Map and i is not present.")
  (see try-ref ref-set get vec-ref map-ref)
  (example """
    (ref [10 20 30] 1)          ; 20
    (ref #map("a" 1) "a")       ; 1
    """)
  (reference """
    The primary method of the @code{Refable} trait, also used by @code{get}
    for nested path lookups.

    Supported collections:
    @itemlist{
      @item{@code{Array} and @code{Vec}: indexed by integer.}
      @item{@code{Map}: indexed by key.}
      @item{@code{string}: indexed by @code{StringCursor}, returning a @code{char}.}
    }
    """))

(:doc try-ref
  (summary "Safely looks up an element by index or key, returning an Option.")
  (arg coll "The collection to index.")
  (arg i "The lookup key or integer index.")
  (returns "(Some element) if present; None if the key is missing or index is out of bounds.")
  (see ref try-get map-try-ref)
  (example """
    (try-ref [10 20 30] 5)      ; None
    (try-ref #map("a" 1) "b")   ; None
    """)
  (reference """
    A non-throwing lookup method on the @code{Refable} trait. Returns @code{None}
    when:
    @itemlist{
      @item{An index into a @code{Vec} is out of bounds.}
      @item{A key in a @code{Map} does not exist.}
      @item{A @code{StringCursor} has reached the end of a string.}
    }

    Note: for .NET @code{Array}, the default implementation calls @code{ref},
    which will throw if the index is out of bounds.
    """))

(:doc ref-set
  (summary "Returns a copy of a persistent collection with an updated key or index.")
  (arg coll "The persistent collection to update (unmodified).")
  (arg i "The index or key to update.")
  (arg x "The new value to associate with i.")
  (returns "A new collection containing the updated value.")
  (raises System.IndexOutOfRangeException "When coll is a Vec and i is out of bounds.")
  (see ref set update vec-set map-set)
  (example """
    (ref-set [1 2 3] 0 9)       ; [9 2 3]
    (ref-set #map("a" 1) "b" 2) ; #map(("a" 1) ("b" 2))
    """)
  (reference """
    The method of the @code{Settable} trait, supporting persistent updates
    for @code{Vec} and @code{Map}. Also used by @code{set} and @code{update}
    when traversing nested key paths.

    For a @code{Vec}, updating an existing index is O(log n); an out-of-range index throws.
    For a @code{Map}, sets an existing key or inserts a new one in O(log n).
    """))

(:doc deref
  (summary "Returns the current value held in a cell, box, or parameter.")
  (arg p "The cell or parameter.")
  (returns "The value currently stored in p.")
  (see try-deref box-ref parameter-ref get)
  (reference """
    The primary method of the @code{Derefable} trait, implemented for
    @code{Box} (calling @code{box-ref}) and @code{Param} (calling
    @code{parameter-ref}). Also used by @code{get} when called without path steps.
    """))

(:doc try-deref
  (summary "Returns the value held in a cell as an Option.")
  (arg p "The cell or parameter.")
  (returns "(Some value) if present, or None if the cell is empty.")
  (see deref)
  (reference """
    The optional access method of the @code{Derefable} trait. The default
    implementation returns @code{(Some (deref p))}, which both @code{Box}
    and @code{Param} use.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Option
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Some
  (summary "An Option holding a value.")
  (arg v "The value.")
  (returns "(Some v).")
  (see None some? option-ref option-ref-or)
  (reference """
    A case of the builtin @code{Option}, which marks presence or absence.
    It is a struct, so building one does not allocate.
    """))

(:doc None
  (summary "The Option that holds nothing.")
  (see Some none? option-ref-or)
  (example """
    (match (map-try-ref m k)
      ((Some v) v)
      (None 0))
    """))

(:doc some?
  (summary "Whether an Option holds a value.")
  (arg opt "The Option.")
  (returns "#t for Some, #f for None.")
  (see none? option-ref))

(:doc none?
  (summary "Whether an Option is None.")
  (arg opt "The Option.")
  (returns "#t for None, #f for Some.")
  (see some?))

(:doc option-ref
  (summary "The value inside a Some.")
  (arg opt "The Option. Must be Some.")
  (returns "The value it holds.")
  (raises System.InvalidOperationException "When opt is None.")
  (see option-ref-or option-value some?))

(:doc option-ref-or
  (summary "Returns the value inside an Option, or a fallback default for None.")
  (arg opt "The Option.")
  (arg fallback "The default value to return if opt is None. Eagerly evaluated.")
  (returns "The value opt holds, or fallback.")
  (see option-value option-ref)
  (example """
    (option-ref-or (map-try-ref settings "port") 8080)
    """))

(:doc option-value
  (summary "Returns the value inside an Option, or a fallback default for None.")
  (arg opt "The Option.")
  (arg fallback "The default value to return if opt is None.")
  (returns "The value opt holds, or fallback.")
  (see option-ref-or)
  (reference """
    The same as @code{option-ref-or}, written in Bjolang with a
    @code{match}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Result
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Ok
  (summary "A Result representing a successful computation.")
  (arg v "The success value.")
  (returns "(Ok v).")
  (see Err ok? result-map)
  (reference """
    A constructor of the builtin @code{(Result %e %a)} type representing a
    successful value. Every @code{#:exceptions} interop call returns a Result.
    A module declaring its own @code{Result} type gets its own @code{Ok}
    and @code{Err}, which shadow these.

    Like any value, a @code{Result} must be explicitly used: matched, propagated,
    or discarded with @code{ignore}.
    """))

(:doc Err
  (summary "A Result representing a failure, holding an error value.")
  (arg e "The error value.")
  (returns "(Err e).")
  (see Ok err? raise)
  (example """
    (match (open-input-file path)
      ((Ok port) (read-all port))
      ((Err e) (raise e)))
    """))

(:doc ok?
  (summary "Whether a Result is Ok.")
  (arg r "The Result.")
  (returns "#t for Ok, #f for Err.")
  (see err?))

(:doc err?
  (summary "Whether a Result is Err.")
  (arg r "The Result.")
  (returns "#t for Err, #f for Ok.")
  (see ok?))

(:doc result-map
  (summary "Applies a transformation function to the value inside an Ok.")
  (arg f "The function applied to the Ok value, returning a new value.")
  (arg r "The Result to transform.")
  (returns "(Ok (f v)) for (Ok v); returns an Err unchanged.")
  (see ok? Ok)
  (example """
    (result-map string-length (Ok "abc"))   ; (Ok 3)
    """)
  (reference """
    To chain a function that itself returns a @code{Result}, use @code{try->}
    instead.

    @code{f} may be either colour. A step that waits has to be a
    @code{(bjoroutine (x) ...)}; written @code{(fun (x) ...)}, the same call
    selects the blocking copy and parks the fiber's thread.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Parameters
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc make-parameter
  (summary "A new parameter: a value that parameterize rebinds for a dynamic extent.")
  (arg v "Its value where nothing has bound it.")
  (returns "A parameter, read with parameter-ref.")
  (see parameter-ref make-cold-parameter deref)
  (example """
    (: verbose? (Param bool))
    (def verbose? (make-parameter #f))

    (parameterize ((verbose? #t))
      (parameter-ref verbose?))   ; #t
    """)
  (reference """
    A parameter is a value, not a function: it is read with
    @code{parameter-ref}, so @code{(Param %a)} is an ordinary type that can
    be passed, stored and returned.

    The first 31 parameters made with @code{make-parameter} are cheap to read
    however many others exist. The count is shared by the whole program,
    the libraries it uses included: the prelude's own effect operations take
    some of the 31. Past that, @code{make-parameter} makes a cold one, as
    @code{make-cold-parameter} does.
    """))

(:doc make-cold-parameter
  (summary "A new parameter that gives up the cheapest kind of read.")
  (arg v "Its value where nothing has bound it.")
  (returns "A parameter, the same as make-parameter's in every other way.")
  (see make-parameter)
  (reference """
    For a parameter that is not read in a loop, so that it does not spend one
    of the 31 ids that stay cheap to read. Worth it for a parameter made per
    request or per connection rather than once at module level. Reading one
    costs nothing extra while it is the only cold parameter bound, and one
    more node access after that.
    """))

(:doc parameter-ref
  (summary "A parameter's value where it is read.")
  (arg p "The parameter.")
  (returns "What the innermost parameterize bound it to, or its initial value.")
  (see make-parameter deref)
  (example """
    (writeln (parameter-ref current-error-port) "failed")
    """))

(:doc parameter-push!
  (summary "Binds a parameter in the current dynamic environment; half of what parameterize expands to.")
  (arg p "The parameter.")
  (arg v "Its new value.")
  (returns "The environment it displaced, for dyn-restore! to put back.")
  (see dyn-restore! parameter-ref)
  (reference """
    @code{parameterize} expands into a push, the body evaluation, and a
    @code{dyn-restore!} of the previous environment in a @code{finally} block.
    Not intended to be called directly.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Equality and hashing
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc eq?
  (summary "Whether two values are the same object.")
  (arg a "The first.")
  (arg b "The second.")
  (returns "#t when a and b are one object; on a value type, when they are equal.")
  (see = structural-equals)
  (reference """
    Object identity, for mutable cells, and not a substitute for @code{=}.
    A value type has no identity to ask about, so on one it compares as
    .NET's default equality does. Keywords and symbols are interned, so on
    them it agrees with @code{=}.
    """))

(:doc clr-eq
  (summary "C#'s == operator, private to std/eq.")
  (arg a "The first.")
  (arg b "The second.")
  (returns "a == b.")
  (see clr-equals =)
  (reference """
    What @code{(std eq)}'s @code{Eq} implementations for the primitive types
    are written with, so that @code{=} on them compiles to the operator.
    Refused in any module but @code{std/eq}: a type's own @code{Eq}
    implementation is what .NET equality is made of, and writing one in
    terms of the other is a loop. Use @code{=}, or
    @code{structural-equals}.
    """))

(:doc clr-equals
  (summary ".NET's default equality, private to std/eq.")
  (arg a "The first.")
  (arg b "The second.")
  (returns "The boolean result of EqualityComparer's Default.Equals.")
  (see clr-eq clr-hash structural-equals)
  (reference """
    Unlike @code{clr-eq} it compiles at a type variable, and it is reflexive
    on NaN, which is why the @code{double} implementation of @code{=} uses
    it. Refused in any module but @code{std/eq}, as @code{clr-eq} is.
    """))

(:doc clr-hash
  (summary ".NET's default hash, private to std/eq.")
  (arg a "The value.")
  (returns "Its hash from EqualityComparer's Default; 0 for null.")
  (see clr-equals structural-hash)
  (reference """
    The hash that goes with @code{clr-equals}, read through the same
    comparer. Refused in any module but @code{std/eq}.
    """))

(:doc structural-equals
  (summary "Field-by-field equality, without the type's own Eq.")
  (arg a "The first.")
  (arg b "The second.")
  (returns "#t when both are of the same runtime type and every field is equal.")
  (see structural-hash = hash-combine)
  (example """
    (impl (Eq Point)
      (defun (= a b) (structural-equals a b))
      (defun (eq-hash p) (structural-hash p)))
    """)
  (reference """
    For an @code{Eq} implementation that wants the field-by-field comparison
    plus one tweak. It never consults the type's own @code{Equals}, which is
    the implementation being written, so it does not loop; each field still
    compares through its own type's equality.

    A primitive, enum or string compares as .NET does. Otherwise the fields
    are found by reflection, those of base types included, and cached per
    type: an escape hatch, not a fast path.
    """))

(:doc structural-hash
  (summary "A hash over a value's fields, without the type's own Eq.")
  (arg a "The value.")
  (returns "A hash that agrees with structural-equals; 0 for null.")
  (see structural-equals hash-combine)
  (reference """
    Folds the fields' hashes in declaration order. It agrees with
    @code{structural-equals} about what is equal, and is not the hash C#
    would have written bit for bit.
    """))

(:doc hash-combine
  (summary "Two hashes folded into one.")
  (arg h1 "The first hash.")
  (arg h2 "The second.")
  (returns "A hash of the pair. Order matters.")
  (see eq-hash structural-hash)
  (example """
    (defun (eq-hash p) (hash-combine (eq-hash (record-ref p x))
                                     (eq-hash (record-ref p y))))
    """)
  (reference """
    What an @code{eq-hash} over several fields folds with.
    @code{System.HashCode.Combine}, so @code{(hash-combine a b)} and
    @code{(hash-combine b a)} differ, and the value differs from one run of
    the program to the next.
    """))

(:doc unhashable
  (summary "The eq-hash of a type that has none: it throws, naming the type.")
  (arg type-name "The type's name, for the message.")
  (returns "Nothing: it always throws.")
  (raises System.InvalidOperationException "Always, saying the type cannot be a Map or Set key.")
  (see hash-combine eq-hash)
  (example """
    (impl (Eq Counter)
      (defun (= a b) (structural-equals a b))
      (defun (eq-hash c) (unhashable "Counter")))
    """)
  (reference """
    A record with a mutable field has no lawful hash: hashing the field
    breaks the rule that a key's hash does not change, and leaving it out
    breaks the rule that equal values hash alike. @code{type/derive} writes
    this for such a type, and it is how a hand-written implementation says
    its type is not a key. It is loud rather than silent because the
    alternative is a @code{Map} that loses an entry the next time the field
    is written.
    """))

(:doc eq-comparer
  (summary "The .NET equality comparer that agrees with = at a type.")
  (returns "An IEqualityComparer for the type the call is used at.")
  (see ord-comparer = eq-hash)
  (example """
    (defun (hashset-empty) (clr-hashset-empty (eq-comparer)))
    """)
  (reference """
    Used when creating collections so that keys are compared using @code{=}
    and hashed using @code{eq-hash}. If the type's own .NET equality matches
    Bjolang's (which is almost always true), it returns .NET's default comparer;
    otherwise, it delegates directly to the type's @code{Eq} implementation.
    """))

(:doc ord-comparer
  (summary "The .NET comparer that agrees with compare at a type.")
  (returns "An IComparer for the type the call is used at.")
  (see eq-comparer compare)
  (reference """
    The ordering counterpart of @code{eq-comparer}: returns .NET's default
    comparer when the type's @code{CompareTo} agrees with its @code{Ord}
    implementation, and delegates to @code{Ord} otherwise.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Failure
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Exception
  (summary "System.Exception, the type every .NET exception derives from.")
  (see raise Err)
  (reference """
    The error type of the prelude's fallible operations, such as
    @code{(Result Exception InputPort)}. The @code{Err} carries the real
    .NET exception, so a handler can test for a more specific type.
    """))

(:doc raise
  (summary "Throws an exception, keeping the stack trace it already has.")
  (arg e "The exception.")
  (returns "Nothing: it never returns, so it fits where any type is wanted.")
  (see Err panic!)
  (example """
    (match (parse s)
      ((Ok n) n)
      ((Err e) (raise e)))
    """)
  (reference """
    The counterpart of @code{try}, which turns the failures it names into
    values: this is how one gets back out. An exception that was caught and
    is raised again keeps the stack trace of where it first failed.

    Its result type is free because it never returns, so it can stand in a
    @code{match} arm whose siblings produce a value.
    """))

(:doc panic!
  (summary "Stops the program with a message.")
  (arg msg "What went wrong, written to the error port.")
  (key #:exit-code "The process's exit code. 1 when not given.")
  (returns "Nothing: it never returns, so it fits where any type is wanted.")
  (see raise)
  (example """
    (when (vec-empty? args)
      (panic! "usage: tool FILE" #:exit-code 2))
    """)
  (reference """
    For a program that cannot go on. It writes @code{msg} and a newline to
    @code{current-error-port}, flushes it, and exits the process. No
    @code{finally} and no scope's cleanup runs.

    At the REPL it does not exit: it throws an exception of its own type,
    which the REPL reports as a panic.
    """))
